    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the spectrum agree that the war in Ukraine has made a rapid increase in defence spending and a concrete push to meet NATO’s 2 percent target a political imperative, and that this fiscal boost must be coupled with structural reforms of the Bundeswehr to improve readiness, procurement speed and integration of new technologies such as drones, cyber‑defence and space assets. At the same time, there is a shared recognition that the conscription debate and the broader transformation of Germany’s security architecture are central to restoring credibility within the alliance, even while commentators differ on the preferred balance between professional forces and citizen‑soldier components. Finally, despite divergent views on specific partners, all outlets stress the necessity of maintaining strong NATO cohesion and a reliable German contribution to collective defence missions abroad.",
      "controversial_points": "German media split between liberal calls for higher NATO spending and conservative warnings of fiscal strain. Debates pit conscription revival advocates against professional army supporters fearing societal militarization. Arms export coverage contrasts economic benefits with ethical concerns over sales to conflict zones. Cyber defense urgency clashes with traditional force emphasis, revealing divergent security paradigms."
